 ## Background
-The method was introduced by Runfeng Li, Mikhail Okunev, Zixuan Guo, Anh Ha Duong, Christian Richardt, Matthew O'Toole, and James Tompkin in the paper "Time of the Flight of the Gaussians: Optimizing Depth Indirectly in Dynamic Radiance Fields" [S1]. It builds upon 3D Gaussian Splatting [13] and C-ToF imaging principles [26, 8]. The work addresses the challenge of reconstructing dynamic scenes from monocular C-ToF cameras, where depth is not directly measured but derived from raw sensor samples [S1]. Previous works like ToRF [1] required additional constraints (camera motion, RGB integration), while F-ToRF [26] struggled with stability in dynamic single-camera settings [S1]. This method leverages the speed of Gaussian splatting to achieve faster optimization while incorporating C-ToF physics [S1].
